# SRG-APP-000315-WSR-000003  -  CAT II
# Remote access to the web server must follow access policy or work in
# conjunction with enterprise tools designed to enforce policy requirements.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000315-WSR-000003
group:           V-206416
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-002314
control_rev5:    AC-17

contribution:    Splunk enforces the remote-access requirements the discussion names from
                 its own configuration: HTTPS on Splunk Web instead of HTTP, and an access
                 control list on each listener it owns in place of the default that accepts
                 connections from anywhere.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL matches ^(1|true)$
check:           web.conf [settings] serverCert non_empty
check:           web.conf [settings] privKeyPath non_empty
check:           web.conf [settings] acceptFrom not_matches (^|[,[:space:]])\*([,[:space:]]|$)
check:           web.conf [settings] acceptFrom not_equals *
check:           server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom not_equals *
check:           web.conf [settings] serverCert not_equals $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/auth/splunkweb/cert.pem
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] acceptFrom https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] privKeyPath https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] serverCert https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Where the organization meets this requirement with an enterprise tool,
                 that directory service, identity provider or remote-access gateway
                 enforces the policy itself - multifactor authentication, device posture,
                 approved access paths - and Splunk only consumes the result.
external:        The organization defines which remote networks and clients are approved;
                 the application stores the resulting ACL but does not determine its
                 contents.
external:        Perimeter firewall, VPN and network segmentation controls on the hosting
                 network carry the remainder of the remote-access policy.
external:        The certificate authority that issues the DoD-approved certificate named
                 by serverCert is managed outside the application.

citation:        Four of the reviewed conditions have to go. authType not_equals Splunk and
                 authSettings non_empty invert the check text, which makes a finding only
                 where "the web server is not configured to enforce these requirements and
                 an enterprise tool is not in place" - an either/or. The discussion's
                 examples of the server enforcing the policy are "implementing IP filtering
                 rules, using https instead of http for communication, implementing secure
                 tokens, and validating users", and native Splunk authentication validates
                 users, so forcing authType away from its documented default of Splunk
                 (authentication.conf.spec:30-33) fails an instance that enforces the
                 policy itself with TLS and ACLs. That is over-reach: delegated
                 authentication is one way to satisfy the requirement, not a condition of
                 it, and it belongs in external dependencies. The sslVersions pair is both
                 out of scope for this requirement and unsatisfiable in a legal case: no
                 TLS version appears in this check or fix text, and the regex requires the
                 literal "tls1.2" although the specification states the version "tls"
                 "selects all versions tls1.0 or newer" and "-" removes a version
                 (web.conf.spec:239-248), so a compliant "tls,-tls1.0,-tls1.1" - tls1.2
                 only - is failed; TLS version floors are the subject of the crypto
                 requirements, not AC-17(1). requireClientCert present demands a key whose
                 default is false and which the check text never asks for
                 (web.conf.spec:151-161). What remains is the application's full share:
                 HTTPS exactly as the product STIG's own fix writes it under SPLK-CL-000290
                 (enableSplunkWebSSL = 1, privKeyPath, serverCert) and the IP filtering the
                 discussion names, where acceptFrom defaults to "*" - "accept from
                 anywhere" - on both of the listeners the application owns
                 (web.conf.spec:1080-1096).
finding:         Splunk does not enforce the remote-access requirements it is capable of
                 enforcing: Splunk Web is not configured for HTTPS with a server
                 certificate and private key, or web.conf [settings] acceptFrom or
                 server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom is unset or left at "*", which accepts
                 connections from anywhere.
